<item>
  <title>Optics Notes#2: The wave equation and the velocity of light</title>
  <dc:creator>Qi Huang</dc:creator>
  <link>https://justcallitaction.cfd/posts/optics-notes/optics-notes-2/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>This note explains how to derive Maxwell’s wave equations.</p>
<section id="the-wave-equation" class="level1">
<h1>The wave equation</h1>
<p>The Maxwell equations describe the electric and magnetic fields as a coupled system. The purpose of this note is to combine them into a single equation for one field. Once this is done, the propagation of light appears directly as a wave phenomenon.</p>
<p>We first record two vector identities that will be used below. For a vector field, the double curl can be written in terms of its divergence and Laplacian: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5C%5C%0A%5B%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7Bu%7D)%5D_i%20&amp;=%20%5Cepsilon_%7Bijk%7D%20%5Cpartial_j%20(%5Cepsilon_%7Bklm%7D%20%5Cpartial_l%20u_m)%0A%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20%5Cepsilon_%7Bkij%7D%20%5Cepsilon_%7Bklm%7D%20%5Cpartial_j%20%5Cpartial_l%20u_m%0A%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20(%5Cdelta_%7Bil%7D%20%5Cdelta_%7Bjm%7D%20-%20%5Cdelta_%7Bim%7D%20%5Cdelta_%7Bjl%7D)%20%5Cpartial_j%20%5Cpartial_l%20u_m%0A%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20%5Cdelta_%7Bil%7D%20%5Cdelta_%7Bjm%7D%20%5Cpartial_j%20%5Cpartial_l%20u_m%20-%20%5Cdelta_%7Bim%7D%20%5Cdelta_%7Bjl%7D%20%5Cpartial_j%20%5Cpartial_l%20u_m%0A%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20%5Cdelta_%7Bil%7D%20%5Cpartial_l%20%5Cpartial_j%20u_j%20-%20%5Cdelta_%7Bim%7D%20%5Cpartial_j%20%5Cpartial_j%20u_m%0A%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20%5B%5Cnabla%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7Bu%7D)%5D_i%20-%20%5B%5Cnabla%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7Bu%7D%5D_i%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A"> <span id="eq-double-curl"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7Bu%7D)%20=%20%5Cnabla%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7Bu%7D)%20-%20%5Cnabla%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7Bu%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B1%7D"></span></p>
<p>The second identity is the product rule for the curl. Here <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?u"> is a scalar field and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D"> is a vector field: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5C%5C%0A%5B%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20(u%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D)%5D_i%20&amp;=%20%5Cepsilon_%7Bijk%7D%20%5Cpartial_j%20u%20v_k%0A%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20%5Cepsilon_%7Bijk%7D%20(%5Cpartial_j%20u)%20v_k%20+%20%5Cepsilon_%7Bijk%7D%20u%20(%5Cpartial_j%20v_k)%0A%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20%5B(%5Cnabla%20u)%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D%5D_i%20+%20%5Bu%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D%5D_i%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A"> <span id="eq-curl-product"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20(u%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D)%20=%20(%5Cnabla%20u)%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D%20+%20u%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B2%7D"></span></p>
<p>The first identity handles the geometry of the vector field. The second becomes important when the material parameters vary from point to point, because <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cepsilon"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmu"> can no longer be moved through a spatial derivative.</p>
<section id="maxwell-equations-in-a-source-free-medium" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="maxwell-equations-in-a-source-free-medium">Maxwell equations in a source-free medium</h2>
<p>We consider a region containing no free current or free charge. Thus <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7Bj%7D=0"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Crho=0">. This does not mean that the electromagnetic field vanishes; it only means that the field is propagating through the region without local sources. In this case Maxwell’s equations are <span id="eq-maxwell-curl"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D%0A%20%20%20%20-%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bc%7D%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20%5Cmathbf%7BD%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%7D%0A%20%20%20%20&amp;=%20%5Cfrac%7B4%5Cpi%7D%7Bc%7D%5Cmathbf%7Bj%7D,%5C%5C%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%0A%20%20%20%20+%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bc%7D%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20%5Cmathbf%7BB%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%7D%0A%20%20%20%20&amp;=%20%5Cmathbf%7B0%7D,%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B3%7D"></span> <span id="eq-maxwell-div"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7BD%7D%20&amp;=%204%5Cpi%5Crho,%5C%5C%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7BB%7D%20&amp;=%200.%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B4%7D"></span> <span id="eq-material"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cmathbf%7Bj%7D%20&amp;=%20%5Csigma%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D,%0A%5C%5C%0A%5Cmathbf%7BD%7D%20&amp;=%20%5Cepsilon%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D,%0A%5C%5C%0A%5Cmathbf%7BB%7D%20&amp;=%20%5Cmu%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D.%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B5%7D"></span> The first curl equation in Equation&nbsp;3, after setting <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7Bj%7D=%5Cmathbf%7B0%7D">, differentiating with respect to time, and using <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BD%7D=%5Cepsilon%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D"> from Equation&nbsp;5, gives <span id="eq-time-ampere"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%7D%0A%20%20%20%20-%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bc%7D%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BD%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%0A%20%20%20%20&amp;=%200,%5C%5C%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%7D%0A%20%20%20%20-%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%7D%7Bc%7D%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%0A%20%20%20%20&amp;=%200,%5C%5C%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B6%7D"></span> This relation connects the time variation of the magnetic vector to the second time derivative of the electric vector. The other curl equation in Equation&nbsp;3 connects the two fields in the opposite direction: <span id="eq-electric-curl"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%0A%20%20%20%20+%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bc%7D%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20%5Cmathbf%7BB%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%7D%0A%20%20%20%20=%200%0A%5Cimplies%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B%5Cmu%7D%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20+%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bc%7D%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%7D%0A%20%20%20%20&amp;=%200%0A%5C%5C%0A%5Cimplies%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B%5Cmu%7D%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20+%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%7D%7Bc%5E2%7D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%20=%200%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B7%7D"></span> Using <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BB%7D=%5Cmu%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D"> from Equation&nbsp;5, divide Faraday’s law in Equation&nbsp;3 by <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmu"> and take its curl. The result contains the same term <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cnabla%5Ctimes%5Cpartial_t%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D"> as Equation&nbsp;6. Substituting Equation&nbsp;6 eliminates <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D"> and produces Equation&nbsp;7, an equation involving only <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D">:</p>
<p>The coefficient <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?1/%5Cmu"> is inside the curl because the permeability may depend on position. Applying the product rule in Equation&nbsp;2 and the double-curl identity in Equation&nbsp;1 gives</p>
<p><span id="eq-electric-wave-pre-div"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B%5Cmu%7D%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20+%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%7D%7Bc%5E2%7D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%20=%200%0A%5C%5C%0A(%5Cnabla%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B%5Cmu%7D)%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20+%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B%5Cmu%7D%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20+%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%7D%7Bc%5E2%7D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%0A%20%20%20%20&amp;=%200%0A%5C%5C%0A-%20%5Cmu%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B%5Cmu%5E2%7D(%5Cnabla%20%5Cmu)%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20+%20%5Cnabla%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%20-%20%5Cnabla%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%0A%20%20%20%20+%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%20%5Cmu%7D%7Bc%5E2%7D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%0A%20%20%20%20&amp;=%200%0A%5C%5C%0A%5Cnabla%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%0A-%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%20%5Cmu%7D%7Bc%5E2%7D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%20+%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Cln%7B%5Cmu%7D)%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%20-%20%5Cnabla%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20&amp;=%200%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B8%7D"></span> The last line of Equation&nbsp;8 is the wave equation for <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D"> before using the divergence condition. Its terms have a simple origin: the Laplacian describes the spatial structure of the field, the second time derivative describes its temporal evolution, and the terms containing <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cnabla%5Cmu"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cnabla%5Ccdot%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D"> account for the material and vector-field constraints.</p>
<p>To remove <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cnabla%5Ccdot%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D">, use the electric part of Gauss’s law in Equation&nbsp;4. Since <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Crho=0"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BD%7D=%5Cepsilon%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D"> from Equation&nbsp;5, <span id="eq-div-product"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20(u%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D)%20&amp;=%20%5Cpartial_i%20(u%20v_i)%20%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20(%5Cpartial_i%20u)%20v_i%20+%20u%20(%5Cpartial_i%20v_i)%20%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D%20%5Ccdot%20(%5Cnabla%20u)%20+%20u%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B9%7D"></span> Therefore, applying Equation&nbsp;9 to <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BD%7D=%5Cepsilon%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D"> from Equation&nbsp;5, <span id="eq-div-electric-displacement"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20(%5Cepsilon%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%20&amp;=%20%5Cpartial_i%20(%5Cepsilon%20E_i)%20%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20(%5Cpartial_i%20%5Cepsilon)%20E_i%20+%20%5Cepsilon%20(%5Cpartial_i%20E_i)%20%5C%5C%0A&amp;=%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20%5Ccdot%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Cepsilon)%20+%20%5Cepsilon%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B10%7D"></span> where <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Crho=0"> has been used from Equation&nbsp;4. Hence Equation&nbsp;4 and Equation&nbsp;10 give <span id="eq-electric-divergence-zero"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20%5Ccdot%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Cepsilon)%20+%20%5Cepsilon%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20=%200%0A%5Ctag%7B11%7D"></span> <span id="eq-electric-divergence"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20%5Ccdot%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Cepsilon)%20&amp;+%20%5Cepsilon%20%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20=%200%20%5C%5C%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20&amp;=%20-%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B%5Cepsilon%7D%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20%5Ccdot%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Cepsilon)%5C%5C%0A%5Cnabla%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20&amp;=%20-%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cnabla%20%5Cln%7B%5Cepsilon%7D%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B12%7D"></span></p>
<p>Substituting Equation&nbsp;12 into Equation&nbsp;8 gives the electric-field wave equation in an inhomogeneous isotropic medium: <span id="eq-electric-wave"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cnabla%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%0A-%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%20%5Cmu%7D%7Bc%5E2%7D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%20+%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Cln%7B%5Cmu%7D)%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D)%20+%20%5Cnabla%20(%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cnabla%20%5Cln%7B%5Cepsilon%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20=%200%0A%5Ctag%7B13%7D"></span> The two additional terms describe the effect of spatially varying material properties. The term containing <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cnabla%5Cln%5Cmu"> comes from the magnetic response of the medium, while the term containing <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cnabla%5Cln%5Cepsilon"> comes from the electric response. They vanish when the corresponding material parameter is constant.</p>
<p>By interchanging the roles of the electric and magnetic fields, and simultaneously interchanging <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cepsilon"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmu">, we obtain the corresponding equation for <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D">: <span id="eq-magnetic-wave"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cnabla%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D%0A-%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%20%5Cmu%7D%7Bc%5E2%7D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%20+%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Cln%7B%5Cepsilon%7D)%20%5Ctimes%20(%5Cnabla%20%5Ctimes%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D)%20+%20%5Cnabla%20(%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D%20%5Ccdot%20%5Cnabla%20%5Cln%7B%5Cmu%7D)%0A%20%20%20%20=%200%0A%5Ctag%7B14%7D"></span> The electric and magnetic wave equations Equation&nbsp;13 and Equation&nbsp;14 are therefore not independent assumptions. They are two forms of the same Maxwell structure, related by the substitutions <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%5Cleftrightarrow%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cepsilon%5Cleftrightarrow%5Cmu">.</p>
</section>
<section id="homogeneous-medium" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="homogeneous-medium">Homogeneous medium</h2>
<p>If the medium is homogeneous, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cepsilon"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmu"> are constants, so <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cnabla%20%5Cln%7B%5Cepsilon%7D%20=%20%5Cnabla%20%5Cln%7B%5Cmu%7D%20=%200">. The equations reduce to</p>
<p><span id="eq-homogeneous-wave"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cnabla%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%20-%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%20%5Cmu%7D%7Bc%5E2%7D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BE%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%20=%200%0A%5Cqquad%0A%5Cnabla%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D%20-%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cepsilon%20%5Cmu%7D%7Bc%5E2%7D%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BH%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D%20=%200%0A%5Ctag%7B15%7D"></span> Both fields are governed by the same wave operator in Equation&nbsp;15. Maxwell’s curl equations in Equation&nbsp;3 still determine how their directions and amplitudes are related; the two equations should not be interpreted as two unrelated waves.</p>
</section>
</section>
<section id="the-velocity-of-light" class="level1">
<h1>The velocity of light</h1>
<p>The standard wave equation for a field <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathbf%7BF%7D"> travelling with speed <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?v"> is <span id="eq-standard-wave"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cnabla%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BF%7D%20-%20%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7Bv%5E2%7D%5Cfrac%7B%5Cpartial%5E2%20%5Cmathbf%7BF%7D%7D%7B%5Cpartial%20t%5E2%7D=0.%0A%5Ctag%7B16%7D"></span> Comparing Equation&nbsp;16 with the homogeneous-medium equations in Equation&nbsp;15 gives the propagation speed <span id="eq-light-velocity"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Av%20=%20%5Cfrac%7Bc%7D%7B%5Csqrt%7B%5Cepsilon%5Cmu%7D%7D.%0A%5Ctag%7B17%7D"></span> It is convenient to define the refractive index <span id="eq-refractive-index"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0An%20=%20%5Csqrt%7B%5Cepsilon%5Cmu%7D,%0A%5Ctag%7B18%7D"></span> so that, using Equation&nbsp;17, <span id="eq-speed-index"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Av=%5Cfrac%7Bc%7D%7Bn%7D.%0A%5Ctag%7B19%7D"></span></p>
<p>In vacuum, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cepsilon=%5Cmu=1"> in these units and the speed is <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?c">. In a material, the electric and magnetic responses change the coefficient of the time-dependent term in Maxwell’s equations, and light consequently travels more slowly. This expression describes the wave speed in a homogeneous, isotropic, nondispersive medium; when <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cepsilon"> or <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmu"> depends on frequency, the phase and group velocities require a separate treatment.</p>


</section>

 ]]></description>
